{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QQSzAomAnmDS3ESTZJ2sas","type":"tool_result","content":"888\t### `builder.setup(ctx)`\n889\t\n890\tRegisters the custom element via `customElements.define` and returns the\n891\telement class. The returned class exposes `.TAG` and `.refs` as static\n892\tmembers, for use in [serverside rendering](#serverside-example).\n893\t\n894\t```ts\n895\tclass ComponentBuilder {\n896\t    setup (\n897\t        fn:(ctx:SetupContext<\n898\t            Props,\n899\t            RefsMap<RefDefs>,\n900\t            ContextsMap<CtxDefs>\n901\t        >) => void\n902\t    ):MicrotagElementClass<RefDefs> {\n903\t}\n904\t```\n905\t\n906\tThe callback function runs once per element instance, and is called with the\n907\tfollowing `context` object.\n908\t\n909\tTeardown waits a microtask. When the element is removed, its cleanups\n910\t(listeners, effects, bindings, `onCleanup` callbacks) run on the next\n911\tmicrotask, and only if it is still disconnected then. So an element that is\n912\tremoved and re-inserted in the same tick (a move) keeps its setup and\n913\tstate, even in browsers without `moveBefore`. After a real removal, cleanup has\n914\thappened once `await Promise.resolve()` returns. If the element is inserted\n915\tagain later, setup runs again.\n916\t\n917\t\n918\t#### `ctx`\n919\t\n920\tThe argument passed to the callback given to `setup`.\n921\t\n922\t```ts\n923\tctx:SetupContext<Props, RefsMap<RefDefs>, ContextsMap<CtxDefs>>\n924\t```\n925\t\n926\t##### `ctx` Methods\n927\t\n928\t| Method | Returns | Description |\n929\t|--------|---------|-------------|\n930\t| `.peek(signal)` | `T` | Read a signal's current value without subscribing, even inside `ctx.effect`. |\n931\t| `.on(target, type, handler, options?)` | `void` | Add a DOM event listener, auto-removed on teardown (a microtask after disconnect). |\n932\t| `.emit(event)` / `.emit(name, detail?, options?)` | `boolean` | Dispatch a pre-built `Event` as-is, or construct and dispatch a bubbling `CustomEvent` from a name. Returns `dispatchEvent`'s result: `false` if a cancelable event had `preventDefault()` called. |\n933\t| `.effect(fn)` | `void` | Run an alien-signals effect that auto-tracks the signals it reads. Auto-disposed on teardown. |\n934\t| `.bind(signal, element, options?)` | `void` | Bind a signal to a DOM element property, auto-cleaned on teardown. |\n935\t| `.onCleanup(fn)` | `void` | Register custom teardown, run a microtask after disconnect. |\n936\t| `.consume(token)` | `T \\| undefined` | Read an optional context value published by an ancestor. See [`withContexts`](#withcontexts). |\n937\t\n938\tSee [Typed Events](#typed-events) for more about `.on` and `.emit`.\n939\t\n940\t##### `ctx.bind`\n941\t\n942\tThe `.bind` method treats the signal as the source of truth. The element\n943\tproperty is set from the signal.\n944\tWith no `options`, the control's element property and write-back event are\n945\tauto-detected from its type:\n946\t\n947\t```ts\n948\t{\n949\t    bind<T> (\n950\t        source:Signal<T>,\n951\t        control:Element,\n952\t        opts?:BindOptions\n953\t    )\n954\t}\n955\t```\n956\t\n957\t| Control | `prop` | write-back `event` |\n958\t|---------|--------|---------------------|\n959\t| `<input type=\"checkbox\">` | `checked` | `change` |\n960\t| `<input type=\"number\">` / `type=\"range\"` | `valueAsNumber` | `input` |\n961\t| Other `<input>` types / `<textarea>` | `value` | `input` |\n962\t| Anything else, e.g. `<select>` | `value` | `change` |\n963\t\n964\tPassing `options` overrides the auto-detected `prop`, and replaces the\n965\twrite-back `event` -- omit `options.event` for a one-way binding. A read-only\n966\tgetter (`() => T`) is also accepted as the `signal` argument, for binding a\n967\tderived value one-way (pass it with `options` and omit `event`)."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f913a53-0f10-4f84-aeba-59a85cf43fa6","uuid":"cb89a386-8d67-4e33-88c5-8f697effb19c","timestamp":"2026-11-24T09:34:51.277Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/nick/code/microtags.form-associated-02-form-associated-link-and-internals/README.md","content":"### `builder.setup(ctx)`\n\nRegisters the custom element via `customElements.define` and returns the\nelement class. The returned class exposes `.TAG` and `.refs` as static\nmembers, for use in [serverside rendering](#serverside-example).\n\n```ts\nclass ComponentBuilder {\n    setup (\n        fn:(ctx:SetupContext<\n            Props,\n            RefsMap<RefDefs>,\n            ContextsMap<CtxDefs>\n        >) => void\n    ):MicrotagElementClass<RefDefs> {\n}\n```\n\nThe callback function runs once per element instance, and is called with the\nfollowing `context` object.\n\nTeardown waits a microtask. When the element is removed, its cleanups\n(listeners, effects, bindings, `onCleanup` callbacks) run on the next\nmicrotask, and only if it is still disconnected then. So an element that is\nremoved and re-inserted in the same tick (a move) keeps its setup and\nstate, even in browsers without `moveBefore`. After a real removal, cleanup has\nhappened once `await Promise.resolve()` returns. If the element is inserted\nagain later, setup runs again.\n\n\n#### `ctx`\n\nThe argument passed to the callback given to `setup`.\n\n```ts\nctx:SetupContext<Props, RefsMap<RefDefs>, ContextsMap<CtxDefs>>\n```\n\n##### `ctx` Methods\n\n| Method | Returns | Description |\n|--------|---------|-------------|\n| `.peek(signal)` | `T` | Read a signal's current value without subscribing, even inside `ctx.effect`. |\n| `.on(target, type, handler, options?)` | `void` | Add a DOM event listener, auto-removed on teardown (a microtask after disconnect). |\n| `.emit(event)` / `.emit(name, detail?, options?)` | `boolean` | Dispatch a pre-built `Event` as-is, or construct and dispatch a bubbling `CustomEvent` from a name. Returns `dispatchEvent`'s result: `false` if a cancelable event had `preventDefault()` called. |\n| `.effect(fn)` | `void` | Run an alien-signals effect that auto-tracks the signals it reads. Auto-disposed on teardown. |\n| `.bind(signal, element, options?)` | `void` | Bind a signal to a DOM element property, auto-cleaned on teardown. |\n| `.onCleanup(fn)` | `void` | Register custom teardown, run a microtask after disconnect. |\n| `.consume(token)` | `T \\| undefined` | Read an optional context value published by an ancestor. See [`withContexts`](#withcontexts). |\n\nSee [Typed Events](#typed-events) for more about `.on` and `.emit`.\n\n##### `ctx.bind`\n\nThe `.bind` method treats the signal as the source of truth. The element\nproperty is set from the signal.\nWith no `options`, the control's element property and write-back event are\nauto-detected from its type:\n\n```ts\n{\n    bind<T> (\n        source:Signal<T>,\n        control:Element,\n        opts?:BindOptions\n    )\n}\n```\n\n| Control | `prop` | write-back `event` |\n|---------|--------|---------------------|\n| `<input type=\"checkbox\">` | `checked` | `change` |\n| `<input type=\"number\">` / `type=\"range\"` | `valueAsNumber` | `input` |\n| Other `<input>` types / `<textarea>` | `value` | `input` |\n| Anything else, e.g. `<select>` | `value` | `change` |\n\nPassing `options` overrides the auto-detected `prop`, and replaces the\nwrite-back `event` -- omit `options.event` for a one-way binding. A read-only\ngetter (`() => T`) is also accepted as the `signal` argument, for binding a\nderived value one-way (pass it with `options` and omit `event`).","numLines":80,"startLine":888,"totalLines":1896}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01QQSzAomAnmDS3ESTZJ2sas","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
